          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true present emotional state. The focal declarative is a clear avowal, independently of whether it is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression states a present emotion; it does not itself promise future conduct, renew an obligation, or undertake a specified relational commitment. Lear's subsequent grant follows the longer answer but does not turn this focal avowal into Goneril's undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is delivered as a composed answer to Lear's formal request, not as a reflex-like or affectively discharged response. The surrounding hyperbole does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force in the exact focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of a loving state. Although it functions rhetorically within Lear's competition for declarations of love, the exact expression has no distinct residual force requiring an O classification.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while announcing that his bounty will correspond to their answers. Goneril, called upon first, answers with the focal avowal and then expands it through elaborate comparisons and hyperbole.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Goneril and therefore has no undertaking for Lear to accept or reject. Lear subsequently awards Goneril territory in response to her answer, but that is his action rather than uptake of a promise contained in “I love you.” The supplied evidence does not settle the sincerity of her professed affection.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied title and text identify the work as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear. No remembered later plot facts are used in the assessment.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt establishes the formal, materially consequential setting and Goneril's verbal avowal, but it does not independently establish whether she actually feels the love she asserts. Cordelia's contrasting comments may cast doubt on the sisters' rhetoric, but they do not conclusively determine Goneril's sincerity.\"}"
        }
